-- THE ONLY IN-TREE COVERAGE OF THE CROSS-MODULE COHERENCE-WARNING PATH (F-3d,
-- #614/#311), and the pin for the DE-DUPLICATION that path needed.
--
-- Two ⊑-incomparable pairs, deliberately placed differently:
--   * `C` -- BOTH impls in lib.mdk. That module's own `checkCoherence` sees them
--     (real span) and so does the whole-graph `globalCoherenceConflict` sweep
--     (span-less, #414). Recording BOTH made `run`/`build` print it TWICE where
--     `check` prints it once; `cohSoftInScope` gives the pair to the per-module
--     sweep, which is the one with the span.
--   * `D` -- one impl in lib.mdk, one in other.mdk. NO per-module sweep can see
--     this pair (each module declares a single `D` impl), so it is reported by
--     `globalCoherenceConflict` ALONE, span-less, naming both owning modules.
--     Before this fixture that whole code path -- the soft half of
--     `globalCoherenceConflict`, `attachEntryWarnOpt`, `prependDiagOpt` -- had zero
--     in-tree coverage; it was verified only by hand probes that left no trace.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived from §3 `match` and §3 ⊑, not captured). Both goals are
-- `… (Pair Int Bool)`:
--   `match(IE, C (Pair Int Bool))` = {`Pair Int a`} alone -- `Pair a Int` does NOT
--   match, since no substitution on `a` makes `Pair a Int` into `Pair Int Bool`
--   (`Int` /= `Bool` at argument 1). A singleton matching set is trivially its own
--   ⊑-minimum, so `inst` selects it: `cname` = 1. Identically for `D`: `dname` = 10.
--   So the program is ACCEPTED under condition (c) and prints 11 -- the declared
--   overlap is real but NO GOAL HERE MATCHES BOTH, which is exactly what "acceptance
--   is per-goal" (§6.1.2) means. The (a) warnings still fire, at the declarations.
--
-- OBSERVED: check/run/build all ACCEPT and print 11, carrying `W-INCOMPARABLE-IMPLS`.
-- Section 5 asserts each message appears EXACTLY ONCE on `run`'s stderr.
import base.{Pair, MkPair, C, cname, D, dname}
import lib
import other

main = println (cname (MkPair 1 True) + dname (MkPair 1 True))
